Smith-Magenis syndrome
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Inverted sleep-wake cycle, Self-injurious behaviours/Polyembolokoilamania, Broad square face, Moderate intellectual disability

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-044
Reason for Visit: Acute behavioral crisis, sleep deprivation, and self-inflicted foreign body insertion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an 18-year-old male with Smith-Magenis syndrome experiencing a severe, explosive temper tantrum at 3:00 AM, during which he aggressively inserted a plastic bead deep into his right ear canal and began violent head-banging. Assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the imminent risk of tympanic membrane rupture, severe self-inflicted traumatic brain injury, and the need for immediate pharmacological de-escalation to break the severe behavioral crisis.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: He has been awake for 24 hours, he is having a massive meltdown, smashing his head, and he shoved a bead deep into his ear.
History of Present Illness: The parents report an acute, severe behavioral decompensation in an 18-year-old male with a documented broad square face and moderate intellectual disability. The patient suffers from severe sleep disturbances characterized by an inverted circadian rhythm. He has been awake for over 24 hours. Two hours ago, exhaustion triggered an explosive temper tantrum and aggressive outbursts. The patient engaged in stereotypic spasmodic upper body squeezing ("self-hugging") before escalating to severe self-injurious behaviour (repetitive head-banging). During the crisis, he exhibited polyembolokoilamania, forcefully inserting a small plastic bead into his right ear canal. He has a hoarse, deep voice, currently screaming in frustration.
Functional Status: Ambulant; dependent on strict caregiver routines for safety and regulation.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate risk of hearing loss/tympanic rupture and permanent neurological injury from head-banging; profound parental sleep deprivation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/90 mmHg | HR: 125 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 65.0 Kg
General: Patient is highly agitated, pacing aggressively, emitting hoarse vocalizations, and attempting to strike his head against the walls.
Physical Exam: Physical exam reveals superficial bruising on the forehead from recent head-banging. Neurological exam reveals profound agitation and impaired judgment, with an apparent decreased pain sensitivity (peripheral neuropathy), as the patient does not guard the bruised forehead. Otoscopic examination of the right ear reveals a small, hard plastic bead firmly wedged in the mid-external auditory canal, occluding the view of the tympanic membrane. The left ear and throat are clear. Cardiovascular exam reveals sinus tachycardia proportional to severe distress.
Environmental Observations: The house is heavily modified with padded corners, indicating chronic severe behavioral challenges.
Medication Reconciliation: Evening dose of Melatonin was administered but was completely ineffective in overcoming the inverted sleep-wake cycle today.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Smith-Magenis syndrome (complicated by acute severe agitation and foreign body in the external auditory canal) - Status: Acute complication
Differentials:

Acute Otitis Media - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause of the agitation, because the foreign body is visually confirmed, there is no fever, and the behavior is a known, classic manifestation of his syndromic sleep deprivation cycle.

Acute Psychosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of hallucinations, the presence of classic syndromic behaviors (self-hugging, polyembolokoilamania), and the direct link to sleep deprivation.

Seizure Disorder (Post-ictal) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the purposeful, albeit maladaptive, nature of the foreign body insertion and the absence of prior loss of consciousness.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Acute physical injury risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of violent head-banging and polyembolokoilamania required urgent medical and behavioral intervention. The objective findings of a foreign body lodged in the ear canal and extreme agitation confirmed an unsafe environment exacerbated by profound sleep deprivation. This assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period because the patient's decreased pain sensitivity meant he would continue head-banging to the point of severe brain injury, and any further manipulation of the ear by the patient could rupture the tympanic membrane. Immediate clinical intervention was necessary to administer chemical sedation safely and perform foreign body extraction.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Olanzapine 10mg (crushed in a small amount of sweet liquid) to rapidly induce sedation and break the severe behavioral crisis. Once the patient was adequately sedated and cooperative (after 45 minutes), successfully removed the plastic bead from the right ear canal using a Jobson Horne probe under direct otoscopic vision. The tympanic membrane was intact and uninjured.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you establish a strict, non-negotiable "lights out and screen-off" policy at 8:00 PM every night, you naturally support his damaged circadian rhythm; therefore, enforcing this routine strictly reduces the severe sleep deprivation that triggers these crises. When you immediately provide a safe, vibrating chew-toy when he begins "self-hugging," you redirect his sensory needs; using this tool proactively prevents him from seeking out dangerous objects to insert into his ears.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to the specialized behavioral support team to review the sleep management protocol.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommended a prescription for a specialized medical-grade weighted blanket to improve sleep onset.
Follow-up: GP Telehealth review tomorrow to assess baseline behavior, inspect the ear canal for delayed swelling, and review regular medication efficacy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Call 000 for police and ambulance support if he wakes up and immediately resumes severe violent behavior that you cannot contain, if he develops clear fluid or blood leaking from the ear, or if he becomes completely unarousable.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Chemical sedation is a crisis intervention; long-term management requires intensive behavioral and sleep therapies.
Clinical Course: The medication will ensure deep sleep for the next 8-10 hours, effectively resetting his immediate behavioral crisis. The ear canal may be mildly tender for 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor the patient's breathing periodically while he sleeps post-sedation. Seek urgent care if he develops a high fever or if he begins to forcefully pull at the ear again upon waking.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: His syndrome severely disrupts his sleep cycle, and the extreme exhaustion caused his brain to lose all impulse control, leading to the dangerous behaviors. The medication acted as a heavy brake to force his brain to sleep and calm down. Removing the bead safely while he was calm prevented him from pushing it through his eardrum and causing permanent deafness.
Actionable Steps: Maintain a completely dark and quiet environment until morning. Implement the strict screen-off protocol tomorrow. Monitor his ear for any bleeding.

